cosphinx 7000/8500 at 36/18 with 18/6, giant mantis 2000/3000 at 12/6 with 6/3, and yeth hound 2000/3000 at 27/12 MC C with 9/3. The columns: normal load is the encumbrance carried at the full rate; maximum load is the greatest load while aloft (a dash means normal is also maximum; anything more and the mount is unable or unwilling to leave the ground, or plummets to the nearest landing spot); the move rate is full/reduced in miles per half-day (a 0 right of the slash means one rate always); the MC when ridden is always one class lower than natural, except E which cannot be reduced; and the stamina is safe turns aloft / turns of rest, with the over-normal-load flying time cut by one turn or one-third of normal, whichever is greater (inapplicable to dash creatures, which cannot fly at all over their normal load). The listed mounts come from the Monster Manual, the Fiend Folio, and Monster Manual II: native to the Prime Material, normally found in a wilderness, and size M or L able to carry through the air; the DM assigns stats for unlisted creatures using the table as a guide. Trained mounts are not easy to find and not cheap; an untrained uncooperative beast can be ridden but the rider is at its mercy. A coerced mount may refuse loads, fly slowly, or insist on rest - full benefit requires willingness. Feeding: most need at least triple rations daily, up to fifty rations for one of the biggest dragon types, and the specialized appetites (the manticore preferring human flesh) may force hunting with the mount. Loads need saddle and containers, ideally distributed evenly on both sides; a load twice as heavy on one side doubles its entire encumbrance value.);
